---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { siteConfig } from "../../config/site";

const prose = "max-w-2xl text-base leading-relaxed text-stone-600";
const sectionHeading =
  "text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-stone-900 sm:text-3xl";
const linkAccent =
  "rounded font-medium text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-700 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-stone-900";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About — ${siteConfig.name}`}
  description="Technical Operations background across production reliability, automation, data systems and cloud infrastructure — plus how I work and what I've been building."
>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 sm:text-5xl">
    About
  </h1>

  <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
    <p class={prose}>
      I work in Technical Operations, which is broad enough as a title that it's
      worth saying what it means to me.
    </p>
    <p class={prose}>
      Most of my work lives between software and the reality of running it:
      production systems, data, integrations, releases, and the little things
      that only become obvious once real users are involved.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="prevention-heading" class="mt-16">
    <h2 id="prevention-heading" class={sectionHeading}>
      From finding problems to preventing them
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
      <p class={prose}>
        A lot of my early work was investigation — backend and database checks,
        release validation, ETL and API flows, and production issues that
        behaved differently outside staging. That meant plenty of SQL, logs, and
        following data through systems until the picture made sense.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        Over time, the interesting question became less "what broke this time?"
        and more "why can this happen at all?" So I started building the things
        that made recurring investigation unnecessary: automated data-quality
        checks, monitoring that surfaced issues immediately, and validation
        before releases rather than after them.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        That shift from finding problems to preventing them has become a
        recurring theme in how I work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="work-i-like-heading" class="mt-16">
    <h2 id="work-i-like-heading" class={sectionHeading}>The work I like</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
      <p class={prose}>
        I like systems that are a little messier than the architecture diagram
        suggests — which is most real systems.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        The work I enjoy most usually starts with something manual, fragile,
        noisy, or dependent on one person remembering the right steps, and ends
        with something automated, observable and easier for everyone to trust.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>Boring is the goal. Boring means it works.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="how-i-work-heading" class="mt-16">
    <h2 id="how-i-work-heading" class={sectionHeading}>How I work</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
      <p class={prose}>
        I'm hands-on by default. I like getting close to the evidence — querying
        the database, checking the API, reading the logs, reproducing the
        behavior — before deciding what the fix should be.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        I also like working across boundaries. Technical Operations naturally
        sits between engineering, product, business, vendors and clients, and
        I've spent a lot of time turning technical constraints into something
        useful for people who don't live in them every day.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        Later, when I led a small technical team, I kept the same approach.
        Leadership added ownership, coordination and mentoring, but I stayed
        involved in releases, incidents, monitoring, validation and automation
        rather than moving away from the technical work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="cloud-heading" class="mt-16">
    <h2 id="cloud-heading" class={sectionHeading}>
      Cloud, infrastructure and what's underneath
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
      <p class={prose}>
        More recently I've been deliberately expanding the infrastructure side
        of that same mindset through hands-on projects: AWS with Terraform,
        Kubernetes, containers, CI/CD, observability, identity and security
        controls, and full create-to-destroy lifecycle automation.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        The <a href="/projects/" class={linkAccent}>projects</a> on this site are
        where that work is demonstrated.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        Underneath it all is a broader systems foundation from training in
        Linux, networking, systems administration, firewalls and cyber security
        including SOC fundamentals.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        I don't treat that as a separate professional identity — it's the
        background that helps when a problem sits one layer lower than expected.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="outside-heading" class="mt-16">
    <h2 id="outside-heading" class={sectionHeading}>Outside the terminal</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
      <p class={prose}>
        Outside work, I spend a fair amount of time in the gym and underwater
        when I get the chance — scuba diving is one of the things I enjoy most.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        I also have a habit of turning random ideas into small technical
        projects, so the terminal never stays completely closed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
